Pre-eclampsia
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
New-onset hypertension >20 weeks, Proteinuria, Severe headache/visual disturbances

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-039
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, unremitting headache, blurred vision, and sudden massive swelling of the face and hands at 34 weeks gestation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and husband
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a third-trimester pregnant patient experiencing a sudden, severe headache unresponsive to analgesia, accompanied by visual disturbances (scotomata) and rapid facial edema. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out severe pre-eclampsia or impending eclampsia, assess blood pressure, and coordinate emergency hospital transfer to prevent maternal stroke or seizure before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have a blinding headache that Panadol won't touch, my vision keeps going blurry with flashing lights, and my husband noticed my face puffed up massively today.
History of Present Illness: The patient, currently at 34 weeks gestation, reports the sudden onset of a persistent, severe headache unresponsive to standard analgesics over the last 12 hours. She describes associated visual disturbances, specifically blurred vision and flashing lights (scotomata). She reports a sudden, significant weight gain over the last three days due to fluid retention, visibly manifesting as non-dependent peripheral edema particularly of the face and hands. She also complains of a new, dull right upper quadrant (epigastric) pain. She notes reduced fetal movements this evening.
Functional Status: Resting in a dark room; unable to tolerate light or noise due to the severe headache.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of eclamptic seizure or maternal stroke; husband is highly anxious.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 175/115 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg (rapid increase)
General: Patient appears highly distressed, photophobic, with marked, "puffy" facial and periorbital edema.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals severe, new-onset hypertension (175/115 mmHg verified bilaterally). Neurological examination demonstrates marked hyperreflexia (4+ patellar reflexes) with 3 beats of sustained ankle clonus, indicating severe central nervous system irritability. Abdominal exam reveals a gravid uterus with right upper quadrant tenderness (liver capsule distention). Point-of-care urinalysis demonstrates 3+ significant proteinuria. Fetal heart tones via Doppler are 130 bpm but with reduced variability.
Environmental Observations: Room is darkened; husband is supportive but panicked.
Medication Reconciliation: Took paracetamol 1g two hours ago with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Pre-eclampsia with impending eclampsia (CNS irritability) - Status: Critical
Differentials:

Migraine - Ruled out by the objective findings of severe hypertension, massive proteinuria, hyperreflexia, and right upper quadrant pain; this is a systemic vascular disease, not a primary headache.

HELLP Syndrome - Highly likely to be co-occurring given the right upper quadrant pain (liver involvement); requires urgent hospital blood work (transaminases, platelets) to confirm.

Gestational Hypertension - Ruled out by the presence of severe systemic features (proteinuria, headache, visual changes, clonus).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Imminent threat of seizure or stroke)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic, dangerous tetrad of severe pre-eclampsia: new-onset severe hypertension (>160/110), significant proteinuria, unremitting cerebral symptoms (headache/visual changes), and marked hyperreflexia (clonus). The presence of right upper quadrant pain and clonus indicates extreme systemic vascular endothelial damage and impending eclamptic seizure. Urgent after-hours intervention was absolutely critical to instantly recognize this hypertensive crisis, secure the patient in a dark, quiet environment to minimize seizure threshold, and activate priority emergency transport to a tertiary obstetric unit for immediate magnesium sulfate infusion and potential emergency delivery. Delaying this care would result in catastrophic maternal or fetal outcomes.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for a "Priority 1 - Severe Pre-eclampsia / Impending Seizure". Instructed the patient to lie in the left lateral decubitus position in a dark, completely quiet room to lower the seizure threshold. Secured a peripheral IV cannula while awaiting paramedics. Advised the husband to clear any sharp objects from the immediate vicinity in case a seizure occurs before EMS arrival.
Positive Behavioral Change: To minimize the risk of triggering a seizure, remain perfectly still with your eyes closed in this dark room; experiencing the reduction in sensory input will help calm your highly irritated nervous system. To ensure the safety of your baby, lie strictly on your left side; understanding that this maximizes blood flow to the placenta will guarantee strict compliance with this position while we wait.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance) activated and on scene.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Direct clinical handover provided to the responding paramedic crew.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently under direct medical supervision awaiting immediate ambulance transport for emergency antihypertensive and anticonvulsant management.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a critical obstetric emergency; the only definitive cure for severe pre-eclampsia is the delivery of the baby.
Clinical Course: The ambulance will take you directly to the delivery suite or ICU. You will be started on an IV drip (magnesium) to prevent seizures and medications to lower your blood pressure, followed likely by induction or cesarean section.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not look at your phone, do not get out of bed, and keep the room completely dark and quiet.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your pregnancy has caused your blood vessels to constrict dangerously, leading to severely high blood pressure. This pressure is causing swelling in your brain (the headache and vision changes), leaking protein into your urine, and irritating your nervous system (the brisk reflexes). You are at imminent risk of having a seizure. You must go to the hospital immediately by ambulance to receive specialized IV medications to protect your brain and lower the pressure, and to prepare for the safe delivery of your baby.
Actionable Steps: Lie still on your left side. Keep your eyes closed. Do not talk unless necessary. Let the paramedics handle all movement.

